\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Measured constellations: individual symbols, not the coherently averaged template. Each symbol is downconverted and integrated over its own symbol interval. Each record is derotated by its own whole-record $m$th-power estimate \eqref  {eq:derotate} and normalised to unit RMS. All records are pooled across the five boards, and 6{,}000 symbols per panel are drawn at random. The spread therefore shows per-symbol capture variability, including device-to-device differences. The 8-QAM panel shows the two rings at the measured radius ratio of $1.89$. No noise has been added.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:constellation}{{2}{6}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Processing chain. The upper path produces ground-truth labels by coherent averaging; the lower path produces the network's input. The paths share the raw capture, and also the word period and the symbol timing $\tau ^{\star }$ estimated from the coherent template. The network's windows are cut on the symbol grid that $\tau ^{\star }$ defines, a dependency not drawn in the diagram. The label path reads the same filtered, resampled waveform from which the windows are cut (Section~\ref {sec:groundtruth}-B). At training and test time, software noise is added to each window first, and the window is then divided by its record's noise-free RMS (Section~\ref {sec:conditioning}). Labels reach the network only through the loss.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:pipeline}{{3}{6}{}{figure.3}{}}
